I chose all negative weights to implement classic proportional‑derivative feedback: push the cart left when the pole tilts right (θ > 0) and also pull it toward the center when the cart deviates from the origin (x > 0). The angle and angular‑velocity gains are larger (‑2.0, ‑0.5) because correcting tilt is more urgent, while the position and velocity gains are modest (‑1.0, ‑0.5) to avoid aggressive drift. A zero bias removes any constant offset that could cause a unidirectional push. This balanced, low‑gain linear controller should reduce saturation and drift, allowing substantially longer episodes and moving toward the global optimum.
