response-og: params[0]: -1.0, params[1]: -7.5, params[2]: -0.5, params[3]: -2.1, params[4]: 0.0  
The current best policies all have a strong positive weight on pole angle (w1≈+7.5) which pushes the cart in the same direction as the tilt, creating a systematic drift (tilt_index ≈ -1, left‑biased). By flipping the sign of w1 (and correspondingly w3) we make the controller corrective: a left‑leaning pole (θ < 0) now yields a rightward force, and vice‑versa, while the angular‑velocity term also damps oscillations. The cart‑position weight (w0) and velocity weight (w2) are kept modestly negative to provide gentle centering and speed damping without saturating the ±3 N limit. No bias is needed because the position term already compensates systematic drift. This sign reversal is expected to drastically reduce monotonic drift and improve long‑term stability, potentially extending episode length well beyond the current ~100‑step local optimum.
